<urn:beads-cache:finding:RBQA-F001> triage:id "RBQA-F001" ; triage:sprint "c-2" ; triage:severity "critical" ; triage:location "crates/bd-cache-sync/Cargo.toml:31" ; triage:source "qa-3 owning reviewer fenced JSON" ; triage:status "open" ; triage:record "{\"carry_forward_from\": \"QA2 boundary specialist; production byte-identical and still open\", \"class\": \"boundary_violation\", \"disposition\": \"open\", \"evidence\": \"At reviewed380f87f3, bd-cache-sync/Cargo.toml31 still directly includes sc-command.workspace=true and cron_log.rs3 imports CanonicalLog, NoopCanonicalLog and SharedCanonicalLog from sc_command::observability. ADR-BDC-0016 decision5 still permits only the upstream published sc-cron contract/logging exception below isolated Python. The implementation does not claim an approved boundary amendment. The corrected provenance guard now detects this edge, which is expected failure evidence, not resolution of the edge. Correlates inherited ARCH-001; upstream/public logging seam remains required. No workaround investigated.\", \"file\": \"crates/bd-cache-sync/Cargo.toml\", \"id\": \"RBQA-F001\", \"issue\": \"The sync crate adds a direct sc-command contract/logging dependency beyond ADR-BDC-0016's approved upstream sc-cron exception.\", \"justification_check\": \"Missing requirement/ADR justification\", \"line\": 31, \"recommendation\": \"Remove the direct sync-to-sc-command edge and preserve scheduler logging through an approved upstream/public port. If the published API cannot support the bridge within current boundaries, resolve that upstream or obtain a separately authorized narrow ADR before changing dependency policy. Do not treat the new Crate Layout row as approval.\", \"related_artifacts\": [\"docs/architecture.md#adr-bdc-0016-adopt-the-sc-runtime-application-framework-and-scheduler\", \"docs/bd-cache-api/requirements.md\", \"docs/bd-cache-py/architecture.md\", \"docs/bd-cache-sync/architecture.md\", \"crates/bd-cache-sync/src/cron_log.rs\", \"ARCH001\"], \"reviewer\": \"ruthless-boundary-qa\", \"severity\": \"critical\"}" .
